    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d261">

                <pb facs="0451" n="193" sameAs="frus:frus1915Supp/pg193"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72112/1872</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Great Britain</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Page</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1915-07-22T18:00:00+00:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">July 22, 1915, 6 p. m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received 7.34 p. m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>2510. Sir Edward Grey has just informed me that he has telegraphed his Ambassador
                    at Washington that the British Government will make cotton contraband before the
                    new crop gets to market. The British commercial attaché at Washington is
                    instructed to confer with the cotton interests and to [come] to some
                    satisfactory working arrangement whereby this country will buy large quantities
                    at a good price. Whatever arrangement is made will be announced when the
                    announcement is made that cotton will be contraband.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Ambassador</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
